MangoPi MQ-Pro
==============

An Allwinner D1: one T-Head C906, which is RV64GC, with 512M of DDR3.
The kernel runs in M-mode, identity mapped, the same way the other
RISC-V boards here do.

What is proved
--------------

Everything in this directory except four addresses and the width of a
UART register has been executed.  Building it with PHYSDRAM, CLINT, PLIC
and UART0 set to what QEMU's -M virt has, the UART read a byte at a time
rather than a word, and Uart0IRQ set to 10, gives a kernel that boots to
an interactive Inferno shell under

	qemu-system-riscv64 -M virt -cpu thead-c906 -bios none -kernel imangopi

which is the same core this board carries.  So l.s, trap.c, clock.c,
main.c, archmangopi.c, uartdw.c, the configuration and mangopiinit all
run; what is not run is the board.

What is not
-----------

The D1's own numbers: DRAM at 0x40000000, the CLINT at 0x14000000, the
PLIC at 0x10000000, UART0 at 0x02500000 with its registers a word apart
and its interrupt on PLIC source 18, and the 24MHz the CLINT counts at.
Those are from the D1 user manual and its device tree, and no board has
been asked whether they are right.

Running it
----------

The kernel wants its DRAM already up, which the boot ROM's SPL does when
it loads off a card, and which xfel does over USB:

	xfel ddr d1
	xfel write 0x40000000 mangopi.bin
	xfel exec 0x40000000

mangopi.bin is the headerless image the mkfile links beside the ELF.
Console is UART0 on the debug header at 115200.

What is missing
---------------

The EMAC, the SD host and the display.  There is no ip stack, no
storage and no draw in the configuration because there are no drivers
for them.
